<!-- Android 9 res/xml/config_webview_packages.xml addition.
     Generated from the ACTUAL signer certificate of input/webview138.apk
     (APK Signing Block v2/v3 pair 0x7109871a, 959-byte DER -> work/V2_cert.der).
     cert SHA-1   : 1F387CB25E0069EFCA490ADE28C060E09D37DD45
     cert SHA-256 : 6FAF3C4140407473400934D117815A21AF1CFEFC5C0BEE61C858BC3D72BA6FE5
     DER length   : 959 bytes (base64: 1280 chars, single line)
     Round-trip base64->DER->base64 asserted on generation.

     GRAMMAR SOURCE: frameworks/base/services/core/java/com/android/server/webkit/
       SystemImpl.java:57-64 (tokens), :84-120 (attributes, required packageName+description),
       :168-181 (readSignatures -> parser.nextText()),
       WebViewProviderInfo.java:42 (new Signature(Base64.decode(text, Base64.DEFAULT))).
     Consequences: 'description' and 'packageName' are MANDATORY (missing either one throws
     AndroidRuntimeException inside the system server -> boot loop); the base64 must be ONE
     continuous line with no internal whitespace and NO space between the tags and the text.

     This is variant A: declares <signature>, therefore per WebViewUpdater.java:506-521 the
     package does NOT need FLAG_SYSTEM. Once framework-res.apk carries this entry, every later
     WebView change is a normal `adb install` needing no platform key.
-->
<webviewprovider description="Google WebView" packageName="com.google.android.webview" availableByDefault="true"><signature></signature></webviewprovider>

<!-- variant B: no <signature> child. WebViewUpdater.java:512-514 then requires
     packageInfo.applicationInfo.isSystemApp(), so the APK MUST live in the system partition
     (/system/app/... with FLAG_SYSTEM). Use this only if the guest's /system is writable and
     the image is being rebuilt for real; it is otherwise the more fragile choice.
<webviewprovider description="Google WebView" packageName="com.google.android.webview" availableByDefault="true" />
-->

